// Practice Test 3 — Scenarios: Code Generation (2), Developer Productivity (4), Claude Code for CI (5), Structured Extraction (6)
window.EXAMS = window.EXAMS || [];
window.EXAMS.push({
  id: 3,
  title: "Practice Test 3",
  questions: [
// ---------------- Scenario 2: Code Generation with Claude Code ----------------
{s:2,d:3,q:"A developer on your platform team created a /deploy-check command file in ~/.claude/commands/. It works great for them, but teammates who pull the repo do not see the command. What is going on, and what is the fix?",
o:["The file is in the user-scoped ~/.claude/commands/ directory; moving it to the repo's .claude/commands/ shares it",
"Slash commands are never shared, so each teammate must recreate the command file on their own machine by hand as well",
"Teammates must run /memory to load new commands, since commands are discovered only after a memory refresh over time",
"The command has to be registered in CLAUDE.md under a commands heading before anyone else can invoke it as a result"],
a:[0],e:"~/.claude/commands/ holds personal, user-scoped commands. Project-scoped commands in the repository's .claude/commands/ are version-controlled and available to everyone who clones or pulls. /memory manages memory files, and CLAUDE.md does not register commands."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your backend has one migrations folder per microservice (services/billing/migrations, services/users/migrations, and so on), and all SQL migrations must follow the same conventions. What is the most maintainable way to apply them automatically?",
o:["A .claude/rules/ file with paths: [“services/*/migrations/**/*.sql”] so it loads whenever matching files are edited",
"A CLAUDE.md inside every migrations directory, each containing a copy of the same conventions for safety's sake",
"A section in the root CLAUDE.md titled Migrations, relying on Claude to infer when the section should be applied",
"A skill named /migration-rules that developers must remember to invoke before editing any SQL migration file"],
a:[0],e:"Path-scoped rules with glob patterns apply conventions to files spread across many directories, which beats duplicated directory-level CLAUDE.md files. Root sections rely on inference, and skills need manual invocation."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your tech lead wants to move testing, API, and deployment conventions out of the root CLAUDE.md into .claude/rules/ files with path scoping. A skeptical teammate asks what you gain. What is the best answer?",
o:["Path-scoped rules load only when matching files are being edited, which reduces irrelevant context and token usage",
"Path-scoped rules are enforced deterministically by the runtime, so Claude can never produce code that breaks them",
"Path-scoped rules are shared with teammates automatically, whereas CLAUDE.md content stays private to each user as well",
"Path-scoped rules can run shell commands to validate code, which a CLAUDE.md file is not permitted to do at all over time"],
a:[0],e:"Path-scoped rules load conditionally when editing matching files, reducing irrelevant context and token usage. They are still guidance (not deterministic enforcement), and a project CLAUDE.md is also shared through version control."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"A new teammate asks why their personal Claude Code preferences are not showing up for the rest of the team, and why the repository's CLAUDE.md seems to apply to everyone. Which explanation of the CLAUDE.md hierarchy is correct?",
o:["User-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md applies only to that user, while project-level CLAUDE.md is shared through git",
"User-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md is synced to teammates automatically whenever they open the same repository locally",
"Subdirectory CLAUDE.md files are ignored unless they are listed in the root CLAUDE.md with explicit @import lines",
"Only one CLAUDE.md can be active at a time, so the most specific file replaces all others for the whole session"],
a:[0],e:"The hierarchy is user-level (~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, personal), project-level (root or .claude/CLAUDE.md, shared via version control), and directory-level (subdirectory CLAUDE.md files). They layer rather than replace one another."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You need Claude to implement a tricky date-range parser for a scheduling feature. It must handle time zones, open-ended ranges, and malformed input. What workflow gives the most reliable progressive improvement?",
o:["Write tests covering expected behavior, edge cases, and performance first, then iterate by sharing the failures over time",
"Have Claude implement the parser first, then ask it to write tests that confirm the behavior it just implemented as a result",
"Describe the parser in prose and accept the first implementation that compiles, adding tests only if bugs appear",
"Implement the parser by hand, then use Claude only to format the code and write the documentation comments later in practice"],
a:[0],e:"Test-driven iteration: write a test suite covering expected behavior, edge cases, and performance first, then iterate by sharing test failures. Tests written after the code tend to confirm what was built rather than what was needed."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You have found three bugs in a payment module: a rounding error, a currency-conversion ordering issue, and a tax calculation that depends on both. Fixing any one changes how the others should be fixed. How should you communicate them to Claude?",
o:["Describe all three issues together in one detailed message, since their fixes interact and must be designed jointly",
"Fix the issues one at a time in separate messages, since sequential iteration is always more reliable than batching",
"Start three separate sessions, one per issue, and merge the resulting changes by hand once all of them are done each time",
"Describe only the most severe issue, since fixing it will usually make the other two interacting issues disappear"],
a:[0],e:"When fixes interact, provide all issues in a single detailed message so the solution can be designed jointly. Sequential iteration suits independent problems, and separate sessions would produce conflicting fixes."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"After a code review you have five small, unrelated problems in different modules: a typo in a log message, a missing null check, an unused import, and two similar nits. None of the fixes affect each other. What is the best approach?",
o:["Address them sequentially, verifying each fix in turn, since independent problems do not need joint reasoning",
"Bundle all five into one long message, since independent issues should always be handled in a single request over time",
"Ignore four of them and fix only the first, since independent issues tend to resolve themselves over time overall",
"Enter plan mode for each issue, since independent issues need an architectural plan before any edit is made over time"],
a:[0],e:"Sequential iteration fits independent problems, because each fix can be verified on its own without joint design. The single-message approach is reserved for interacting issues where the fixes affect each other."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Claude's migration script mishandles rows where the discount column is NULL in your orders table. Your prose explanation (“handle nulls properly”) has not fixed it after two attempts. What should you do?",
o:["Provide a specific test case with example input containing nulls and the exact expected output for those rows",
"Tell Claude that the migration script has a bug somewhere in its null handling and ask it to fix the problem",
"Ask Claude to rewrite the whole migration script from scratch, since the null bug suggests a flawed design",
"Add a note to CLAUDE.md saying that nulls matter, so future sessions will be more careful about null values"],
a:[0],e:"Providing specific test cases with example input and expected output is the most effective way to fix edge-case handling when prose is ambiguous. “Handle nulls properly” does not say what the correct output is."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You are triaging four tasks for Claude Code on a notifications project. Which one benefits most from plan mode?",
o:["Adding real-time notifications, where WebSockets, polling, and server push are all viable with different tradeoffs",
"Adding a single date validation conditional to one form handler where the expected behavior is already specified",
"Fixing a typo in an error message string that appears once in a single file the developer has already found each time",
"Renaming a local variable inside one function so that its name better reflects the value that it currently holds as well"],
a:[0],e:"Plan mode is designed for tasks with multiple valid approaches, architectural decisions, and multi-file changes. Single, well-scoped changes (a validation conditional, a typo, a rename) are direct-execution tasks."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"A teammate says plan mode “just slows things down” and wants to skip it for a 30-file refactor of the data access layer. What is the strongest counterpoint?",
o:["Plan mode explores the code and settles the design before any edits, preventing costly rework from late surprises",
"Plan mode runs every edit in a sandbox and rolls back automatically, so mistakes never reach the actual repository",
"Plan mode uses a larger model than direct execution, so it produces higher quality code on the first attempt made",
"Plan mode disables all tools except Write, which keeps the agent focused on editing files instead of reading them"],
a:[0],e:"Plan mode enables safe codebase exploration and design before committing to changes, preventing costly rework when dependencies are discovered late. It does not sandbox edits, switch models, or restrict tools to Write (it is read-oriented)."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"A teammate new to Claude Code asks why the Explore subagent keeps appearing during discovery on your monorepo. What is its main purpose?",
o:["It runs verbose discovery in an isolated context and returns a summary, preserving the main conversation's context",
"It opens an interactive file browser so the developer can explore the repository visually outside of the session",
"It is a plan mode setting that disables edits, so the main agent can only read files while exploring them",
"It indexes the full codebase into a vector database that the main agent can query with natural language later"],
a:[0],e:"The Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output and returns summaries, preserving the main conversation's context and preventing context exhaustion during multi-phase tasks."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your /brainstorm-approaches skill generates many alternative designs and lots of exploratory reasoning. Afterward, the main session is cluttered and Claude sometimes mixes rejected ideas into the implementation. What should you change?",
o:["Set context: fork on the brainstorming skill so its exploratory content stays in an isolated sub-agent context over time",
"Set allowed-tools to none on the skill so that it cannot generate exploratory content beyond a short final list",
"Move the skill content into CLAUDE.md so it is always available and does not need to be invoked as a skill",
"Run /compact after every brainstorming session so that the exploratory content gets removed from the context"],
a:[0],e:"context: fork runs a skill in an isolated sub-agent context, which suits verbose output and exploratory content like brainstorming alternatives and prevents pollution of the main conversation. /compact summarizes but leaves remnants, and allowed-tools does not limit reasoning."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your CLAUDE.md includes a 400-line release process that your team needs about once a month, but it loads in every session and crowds out more relevant context. What is the best change?",
o:["Move the release process into a skill invoked on demand, keeping CLAUDE.md for standards that apply universally",
"Keep it in CLAUDE.md but compress it into fewer lines by removing all of the examples and explanatory sections each time",
"Move it into ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md for the release manager only, so the rest of the team never needs to see it over time",
"Move it into .mcp.json as a resource, so Claude Code loads it automatically when it detects release-related words"],
a:[0],e:"Skills are for on-demand, task-specific workflows, and CLAUDE.md is for always-loaded universal standards. Compressing loses useful detail, and user-level placement hides the process from other potential release managers."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your frontend team adds .claude/rules/testing.md with YAML frontmatter paths: [“**/*.test.tsx”]. A developer asks when those conventions will be applied. What is the correct answer?",
o:["When Claude works with files matching **/*.test.tsx, regardless of which directory the test files are located in",
"At the start of every session regardless of which files are edited, since all rule files are always loaded as well",
"Only when the developer invokes it as a slash command named after the rule file, such as /testing in this case over time",
"Only when Claude edits files in the .claude/rules/ directory itself, since rules apply to their own directory overall"],
a:[0],e:"The paths frontmatter field holds glob patterns for conditional activation, so the rule loads when working with matching files anywhere in the codebase. Rules are not slash commands, and the glob is not relative to the rules folder."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You want the root CLAUDE.md of your API repo to pull in the contents of docs/api-standards.md without copying the text into it. What is the correct approach?",
o:["A line such as @docs/api-standards.md inside CLAUDE.md, which pulls that file's contents into the loaded memory",
"A line such as #include “docs/api-standards.md” inside CLAUDE.md, mirroring the C preprocessor include syntax as well",
"A frontmatter key imports: [docs/api-standards.md] at the top of CLAUDE.md, which the loader parses on startup each time",
"A symlink from CLAUDE.md to docs/api-standards.md, since Claude Code can only read one memory file per project"],
a:[0],e:"CLAUDE.md supports @import syntax (for example @docs/api-standards.md) for referencing external files, keeping configuration modular. #include and an imports frontmatter key are not supported mechanisms."},

// ---------------- Scenario 4: Developer Productivity with Claude ----------------
{s:4,d:1,q:"You resume yesterday's named session analyzing the payments module. Since then, a teammate modified two of the files you analyzed. What is the most efficient way to proceed?",
o:["Tell the resumed session exactly which files changed and how, so it can re-analyze those files in a targeted way",
"Say nothing, since a resumed session automatically detects file changes and refreshes all earlier tool results over time",
"Ask the session to re-explore the entire codebase from scratch, since any change invalidates all prior analysis each time",
"Delete the session and start over with no context, since resumed sessions cannot work with modified files at all each time"],
a:[0],e:"When resuming after code modifications, inform the agent about the specific changed files for targeted re-analysis rather than full re-exploration. Resumed sessions do not automatically know their earlier tool results are stale."},

{s:4,d:1,q:"Last week's investigation session analyzed the checkout module, which has since been heavily refactored: most file paths and class names have changed. You need to continue the investigation. What is the most reliable approach?",
o:["Start a new session and inject a structured summary of the earlier findings, since the old tool results are stale",
"Resume the old session, since resumption is always more reliable than starting fresh with a written summary overall",
"Resume the old session and ask it to ignore any tool results it suspects may be out of date after the refactor overall",
"Start a new session with no summary at all, since any carried-over findings could be contaminated by stale data each time"],
a:[0],e:"Choose resumption when prior context is mostly valid, and start fresh with an injected summary when prior tool results are stale. The model cannot reliably tell which results are outdated, and a clean start without the summary throws away still-useful conclusions."},

{s:4,d:1,q:"An engineer asks the agent to “add comprehensive tests to our legacy billing codebase,” which has no test suite today. What decomposition strategy is most effective?",
o:["Map the codebase structure, identify high-impact areas, then build a prioritized plan that adapts as it goes over time",
"Generate tests for every file in alphabetical order, so that coverage grows evenly and no module is overlooked",
"Ask for comprehensive tests for the entire codebase in one prompt, so the agent sees everything at once",
"Write tests only for the files that changed most recently, since older code has already proven that it works"],
a:[0],e:"Open-ended tasks should be decomposed by first mapping structure and identifying high-impact areas, then creating a prioritized plan that adapts as dependencies are discovered. Alphabetical order ignores impact, and one giant prompt dilutes attention."},

{s:4,d:5,q:"While investigating a bug, the main agent's context fills up with output from searching for test files, tracing the refund flow, and listing config files. What design keeps the main agent effective?",
o:["Spawn subagents to investigate specific questions, with the main agent keeping only high-level coordination overall",
"Have the main agent do all investigation itself, since delegated subagents lose the context needed to be useful as well",
"Spawn one subagent per file in the repository so that every file is analyzed in a fully isolated context window",
"Disable tool output entirely for the main agent and rely on the model's prior knowledge of similar codebases as well"],
a:[0],e:"Spawn subagents for specific questions (for example “find all test files” or “trace refund flow dependencies”) so verbose exploration output stays isolated while the main agent preserves high-level coordination."},

{s:4,d:5,q:"Phase one of a codebase investigation (mapping the architecture) is complete, and phase two will spawn subagents to analyze specific subsystems. How should the phase-two subagents be initialized?",
o:["Summarize the key findings from phase one and inject that summary into the initial context of each phase-two subagent",
"Pass the full raw transcripts from phase one to every phase-two subagent, so that no detail is lost along the way over time",
"Start the phase-two subagents with no context, so that they form independent views uninfluenced by phase one over time",
"Have phase-two subagents read phase-one subagent memory directly, since subagents share memory across phases each time"],
a:[0],e:"Summarize key findings from one exploration phase before spawning subagents for the next, injecting the summaries into their initial context. Raw transcripts waste context, and subagents do not share memory."},

{s:4,d:5,q:"You are monitoring a long codebase-exploration session with your agent. Which symptom most strongly indicates context degradation?",
o:["The agent gives inconsistent answers and refers to typical patterns instead of the specific classes it found earlier",
"The agent starts calling Grep more often than Read, which shows it has lost track of the files it already read",
"The agent asks clarifying questions about the task, which shows that it has lost the original instructions given as well",
"The agent's responses become shorter, which shows that it is running out of output tokens on each of its turns"],
a:[0],e:"Context degradation in extended sessions shows up as inconsistent answers and references to “typical patterns” rather than the specific classes discovered earlier. Countermeasures include scratchpad files, subagent delegation, and /compact."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your agent must (1) create a brand-new config file from a template and (2) change one specific line in an existing 2,000-line file where the target text is unique. Which tools fit best?",
o:["Write for creating the new file, and Edit for the one-line change, since Edit targets unique text matches",
"Edit for creating the new file, and Write for the one-line change, since Write is safer for existing files",
"Bash with echo for both operations, since shell commands are faster than any of the built-in file tools",
"Write for both operations, since Edit is deprecated and Write is always the preferred way to modify files"],
a:[0],e:"Write handles full file creation, and Edit handles targeted modifications using unique text matching. Rewriting a 2,000-line file for a one-line change risks unintended changes, and Read plus Write is the fallback only when Edit's anchor is not unique."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"While building an MCP server for your team, a junior engineer asks what the isError flag in a tool result is for, since the tool already returns an error message. What is the best explanation?",
o:["It tells the agent that the tool call failed, so the agent can reason about recovery rather than assume success overall",
"It is set by the agent on its own response to tell the user that a tool returned an unexpected or malformed value",
"It is a server option in .mcp.json that disables a tool after it fails more than a set number of times in a row",
"It is an HTTP status header that MCP clients use to decide when to automatically reconnect to an unavailable server"],
a:[0],e:"The MCP isError flag communicates tool failures back to the agent so it can make recovery decisions. Paired with structured error metadata (category, retryability), it enables appropriate handling."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your agent has a single analyze_document tool used for extracting data points, summarizing, and checking claims against sources. Results are inconsistent and the agent often passes the wrong kind of request. What is the best redesign?",
o:["Split it into purpose-specific tools with defined input and output contracts, such as extraction and summarization",
"Keep one generic tool but add a mode parameter with a free-text value describing the kind of analysis wanted each time",
"Merge it with every other analysis tool into a single universal tool, so the agent never has to choose between them",
"Keep the generic tool and add few-shot examples showing what the tool's output looks like in a range of cases over time"],
a:[0],e:"Split generic tools into purpose-specific tools with defined input and output contracts, for example extract_data_points, summarize_content, and verify_claim_against_source. A free-text mode parameter keeps the ambiguity, and merging makes it worse."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your agent has analyze_content and analyze_document tools with nearly identical descriptions, and requests about web search results are often routed to analyze_document. What is the best fix?",
o:["Rename analyze_content to extract_web_results and rewrite its description to make its web-specific purpose clear",
"Keep both names but add the word IMPORTANT to the beginning of the analyze_document description for emphasis",
"Randomize the order in which the two tools are listed on each request so that neither tool is chosen more often",
"Remove analyze_document entirely, since having two analysis tools will always cause the agent to choose wrongly overall"],
a:[0],e:"Ambiguous or overlapping tool descriptions cause misrouting. Rename tools and update descriptions to eliminate functional overlap (for example analyze_content becomes extract_web_results with a web-specific description)."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"You are reviewing a teammate's MCP server before launch, and its tool descriptions are one short line each. What should a well-written tool description include to maximize reliable tool selection?",
o:["Input formats, example queries, edge cases, and clear boundaries explaining when to use it versus similar tools",
"Only the tool's name repeated in plain English, since longer descriptions consume tokens and confuse the model as well",
"The implementation language and internal source code of the tool, so the model understands how it operates each time",
"A list of the users who are permitted to call the tool, so that the model can enforce access control on its own"],
a:[0],e:"Tool descriptions are the primary mechanism LLMs use for tool selection. Good descriptions include input formats, example queries, edge cases, and boundary explanations versus similar tools."},

{s:4,d:4,q:"A developer new to the Claude API is configuring tool_choice for an agent and asks what the three main options do. Which statement is correct?",
o:["“auto” lets the model reply with text instead, “any” requires some tool call, and a named tool forces that tool",
"“auto” forces a tool call, “any” allows plain text replies, and a named tool makes that tool the preferred option",
"“auto” and “any” behave the same, while a named tool forces the model to call that tool before any other",
"“auto” requires a tool call, “any” requires every listed tool be called, and a named tool disables all the others"],
a:[0],e:"“auto”: the model may call a tool or return text. “any”: the model must call a tool but chooses which. {“type”: “tool”, “name”: …}: the model must call that specific tool."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Users report the error “Invalid session token” appearing in your web app. An engineer asks the agent to find where it is raised. What should the agent do first?",
o:["Grep for the exact error message text across the codebase to find the file and line where it is raised as well",
"Glob for files named *error* across the codebase, since error messages are usually defined in error files each time",
"Read the main entry point file and follow every import until the error message is found in some module",
"Bash with find to list every file, then read the list to guess which module most likely throws the error"],
a:[0],e:"Grep is the tool for searching code content, such as locating error messages, function names, or imports across the codebase. Glob only matches file names, and the other approaches are slow and indirect."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your team's internal API MCP server needs an API key. Which .mcp.json configuration follows recommended practice for a project-scoped server?",
o:["“env”: { “API_KEY”: “${INTERNAL_API_KEY}” } in .mcp.json, with each developer setting the variable locally",
"“env”: { “API_KEY”: “sk-live-4f9a...” } in .mcp.json, with the repository restricted to the core team only overall",
"“env”: { “API_KEY”: “$(cat ~/.secrets/key)” } in .mcp.json, executed by a shell when the server is started",
"“env”: { “API_KEY”: “{{vault.internal_api_key}}” } in .mcp.json, resolved by a built-in secrets manager overall"],
a:[0],e:".mcp.json supports environment variable expansion with ${VAR} syntax, keeping credentials out of version control. Hardcoded secrets leak, and shell substitution and vault templating are not supported .mcp.json mechanisms."},

{s:4,d:1,q:"Your security team requires a guarantee that the agent can never run destructive shell commands like rm -rf or git push --force, even if the model decides to. What is the most reliable mechanism?",
o:["A PreToolUse hook on Bash that inspects the command and blocks destructive patterns before they ever execute overall",
"A PostToolUse hook on Bash that detects destructive commands after they run and reports them to the developer over time",
"A system prompt instruction listing forbidden commands, which the model will follow reliably in every session",
"A CLAUDE.md note asking the agent to be careful with Bash, which Claude Code turns into an enforced rule set"],
a:[0],e:"Hooks that intercept outgoing tool calls provide deterministic enforcement, and a PreToolUse hook blocks the command before execution. PostToolUse is too late, and prompt and CLAUDE.md guidance are probabilistic."},

// ---------------- Scenario 5: Claude Code for Continuous Integration ----------------
{s:5,d:3,q:"You need to add a Claude Code security review as a non-interactive step in your CI pipeline. Which command is appropriate?",
o:["claude -p “Review the diff for security issues” --output-format json, which prints structured output and then exits",
"claude --headless “Review the diff for security issues” --format=json, which suppresses the interactive terminal UI",
"claude --batch “Review the diff for security issues” --json-output, which queues the review as an asynchronous job",
"CLAUDE_CI=1 claude “Review the diff for security issues” --quiet, which switches the CLI into its pipeline mode"],
a:[0],e:"-p (--print) is the documented non-interactive mode: it processes the prompt, prints the result, and exits, and --output-format json gives structured output. --headless, --batch, and CLAUDE_CI are not real Claude Code options."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"A teammate asks why your pipeline uses a separate Claude instance to review generated code instead of asking the generating session to review its own work with extended thinking. What is the best reason?",
o:["The generator retains its reasoning context, so it tends not to question its own decisions, even with more thinking",
"Extended thinking is unavailable in CI, so a second instance is the only way to get a review in a pipeline as a result",
"An independent instance always uses a larger model, which is the reason that it catches more of the subtle issues overall",
"The generating session's context is cleared after generation, so it cannot see the code that it just produced"],
a:[0],e:"Self-review limitation: a model retains reasoning context from generation, making it less likely to question its own decisions. Independent instances without that context catch subtle issues more effectively than self-review instructions or extended thinking."},

{s:5,d:1,q:"Your CI review must check every pull request for the same three aspects each time: security, performance, and API compatibility. Which decomposition pattern fits best?",
o:["Prompt chaining: run focused sequential passes for each aspect, since the review steps are known and predictable",
"Dynamic decomposition: let the agent invent new subtasks at each step, since the review aspects are unknown as well",
"A single prompt covering every aspect at once, since splitting reviews always introduces contradictory findings overall",
"Random sampling of aspects on each run, so that over many runs every review aspect is eventually covered each time"],
a:[0],e:"Prompt chaining (fixed sequential pipelines) suits predictable multi-aspect reviews. Dynamic decomposition is for open-ended investigations where subtasks depend on what is discovered."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your manager wants to move the blocking pre-merge review to the Message Batches API to save 50% on cost. What is the core reason this is a poor fit?",
o:["Batch has no guaranteed latency and may take up to 24 hours, which is unacceptable when developers are waiting",
"Batch does not support code review prompts, since it accepts only short single-sentence prompts in each request",
"Batch results are returned without any identifiers, so findings cannot be matched to the right pull request",
"Batch costs more than synchronous requests for small workloads, which makes it uneconomical for pull requests as well"],
a:[0],e:"The Batches API has up to a 24-hour processing window with no latency SLA, which is fine for overnight jobs but inappropriate for blocking workflows. Results are correlated with custom_id, and batch is cheaper (50%), not costlier."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"You are building a nightly batch job that submits 3,000 review requests, one per merged PR. A teammate asks why every request needs a custom_id. What is the right answer?",
o:["It lets you correlate each result with its request, since batch results may not come back in submission order",
"It sets the priority of each request, so that important requests in the batch are processed by the API first",
"It chooses which model processes each request, since a batch can route requests to different model families",
"It authenticates each request separately, since every request in a batch requires its own unique credentials"],
a:[0],e:"custom_id correlates batch request and response pairs, and it is also how you identify failed items to resubmit selectively. It does not set priority, model, or credentials."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"You are rewriting your CI review prompt to cut false positives without missing real problems. Which excerpt is most likely to do that?",
o:["Report: logic bugs, security flaws, data loss risks. Skip: style nits, naming preferences, and local conventions",
"Report anything that might be a problem, but only when you are quite sure it is a genuine issue for this codebase",
"Be conservative and thoughtful. Avoid false positives. Developers are busy and dislike irrelevant review comments",
"Report all issues you find, ranked by your confidence, and the developers will decide which of them are relevant each time"],
a:[0],e:"Specific review criteria that define which issues to report versus skip beat confidence-based filtering or general exhortations like “be conservative,” which do not reliably improve precision."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your CI review has one noisy category and several accurate ones. A teammate argues the noisy category is harmless because the other findings are right. How do high false-positive rates in one category typically affect developers?",
o:["A noisy category erodes trust in all findings, including the accurate ones in other categories of the review over time",
"False positives have no effect on trust as long as the accurate findings outnumber the false ones in each review",
"Developers trust each category independently, so a noisy category affects only the trust in that one category over time",
"False positives are harmless in CI, since developers dismiss them quickly with no lasting effect on behavior"],
a:[0],e:"High false-positive categories undermine confidence in the accurate categories too, and developers start ignoring everything. That is why temporarily disabling noisy categories can be the right move."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"A teammate wants to give the review agent an exhaustive rulebook of every code pattern to flag or ignore. You propose few-shot examples instead. What is your strongest argument?",
o:["Few-shot examples let the model generalize judgment to novel patterns, rather than matching only pre-listed cases",
"Few-shot examples are cheaper than rules in tokens, since examples are compressed before they reach the model overall",
"Few-shot examples guarantee deterministic output, since the model copies the example closest to each new input over time",
"Few-shot examples replace the need for any instructions, so the system prompt can hold examples and nothing else as well"],
a:[0],e:"Few-shot examples enable the model to generalize judgment to novel patterns rather than matching only pre-specified cases. They are not compressed, do not guarantee determinism, and work alongside instructions."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"You are adding few-shot examples to the review prompt to improve decisions on borderline cases. What is the recommended design?",
o:["Two to four targeted examples of ambiguous cases, each showing why one action was chosen over the alternatives",
"Twenty or more examples of clear-cut cases, so the model sees a large volume of correct decisions to imitate overall",
"One example of a perfect review, since a single strong example is enough to establish the expected behavior over time",
"Examples of only the failure cases, without any explanation, so the model infers the right behavior by itself overall"],
a:[0],e:"Create 2 to 4 targeted few-shot examples for ambiguous scenarios that show the reasoning for choosing one action over plausible alternatives. Clear-cut examples teach little about borderline judgment."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Tests generated in your CI reach decent line coverage but consistently miss untested branches, such as error paths and else clauses. What is the most effective prompt improvement?",
o:["Add few-shot examples that show identifying untested branches, such as an uncovered else path, and testing them",
"Ask for 100% line coverage, since full line coverage automatically guarantees that every branch is tested as well",
"Generate tests only for functions without branches, since branching functions are too complex for generation",
"Increase the number of generated tests per function, since more tests will eventually cover all of the branches overall"],
a:[0],e:"Few-shot examples are effective for demonstrating ambiguous-case handling such as branch-level coverage gaps. Line coverage does not imply branch coverage, and more tests of the same kind do not target the gap."},

{s:5,d:3,q:"Your CI script posts review findings as inline PR comments. A teammate asks what the --json-schema flag does when used with claude -p. What is the correct answer?",
o:["It constrains the final output of a -p run to match the provided JSON schema, producing validated data as well",
"It validates the repository's JSON files against a schema before Claude Code starts reviewing the pull request",
"It defines the schema of the MCP servers that Claude Code is allowed to connect to during the CI pipeline run over time",
"It converts CLAUDE.md into JSON so that the CI system can parse the project's review criteria programmatically"],
a:[0],e:"--output-format json with --json-schema enforces structured output in CI, producing machine-parseable findings (for example for posting inline PR comments)."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"A teammate proposes running three independent reviews of each PR and posting only the issues flagged by at least two of the three. What is the main drawback?",
o:["Real bugs that are caught only intermittently would be suppressed, so consensus filtering lowers bug detection",
"Three reviews triple the cost, which is the only drawback, since consensus filtering otherwise improves precision",
"The three reviews would be identical, since the model is deterministic, so the extra passes add nothing at all overall",
"Consensus filtering is not supported by --output-format json, so the results of each run cannot be compared"],
a:[0],e:"Requiring consensus suppresses real bugs that are only caught intermittently. Better precision levers are explicit criteria, few-shot examples, and a focused multi-pass architecture."},

{s:5,d:3,q:"Your CI job runs Claude Code on a hosted runner. A teammate asks how the run receives your project's review criteria and testing conventions. What is the correct answer?",
o:["Through the project's CLAUDE.md, which Claude Code loads in CI just as it does in an interactive session each time",
"Through each developer's ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, which CI runners download from developer machines before a job",
"Only through the -p prompt string, since CLAUDE.md files are ignored whenever Claude Code runs non-interactively",
"Through a .claude/ci.yaml file that defines review criteria in a format specific to CI pipeline integration"],
a:[0],e:"CLAUDE.md is the mechanism for providing project context (testing standards, fixture conventions, review criteria) to CI-invoked Claude Code. There is no special ci.yaml, and user-level files are not on CI runners."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your review bot's findings are noisy, and engineering leadership asks for the two most direct changes to raise precision. Which TWO should you recommend? (Select TWO.)",
o:["Define specific categorical criteria for what to report and what to skip, instead of confidence-based filtering",
"Add few-shot examples that distinguish acceptable code patterns from genuine issues, with reasoning for each one",
"Add the instruction “only report high-confidence findings” to the system prompt as the main precision lever",
"Increase temperature so that the review explores more possibilities and filters out weaker findings by itself",
"Require consensus between three independent runs before any finding is allowed to be posted on the pull request"],
a:[0,1],e:"Explicit categorical criteria and few-shot examples distinguishing acceptable patterns from genuine issues are the recommended precision levers. Confidence instructions do not reliably help, temperature does not filter, and consensus suppresses intermittently caught bugs."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Review findings from claude -p will be posted as inline PR comments by a script. Which output schema design is best?",
o:["Required file, line, severity enum, issue, and suggested_fix fields, plus a nullable field for optional context",
"A single free-text summary field, so that the model can format findings however it believes is most readable over time",
"Every field optional, so that the model never fails validation even when it cannot determine the line number as well",
"A nested object per file containing raw markdown, which the CI script splits into comments using headings"],
a:[0],e:"Downstream automation needs reliable fields: required location (file, line), a severity enum, the issue, and a suggested fix, plus nullable fields for genuinely optional data. Free text and all-optional schemas break the inline-comment integration."},

// ---------------- Scenario 6: Structured Data Extraction ----------------
{s:6,d:4,q:"You are extracting ingredients from recipes for a meal-planning app. Measurements like “a pinch,” “two handfuls,” and “a splash” lead the model to invent precise gram values. What is the most effective fix?",
o:["Add few-shot examples showing how informal measurements are extracted, including when to keep the original text",
"Instruct the model to convert all informal measurements into grams using its best estimate for each ingredient",
"Mark measurement fields as required numbers so the model must supply a numeric value for every ingredient line",
"Remove recipes with informal measurements from the dataset, since they cannot be extracted with any accuracy each time"],
a:[0],e:"Few-shot examples are effective for reducing hallucination in extraction tasks, such as handling informal measurements. Requiring numbers or demanding estimates encourages fabrication."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Your citation extractor works on papers with inline citations but misses most references in papers that use a numbered bibliography section. What is the best fix?",
o:["Add few-shot examples showing extraction from both inline citations and bibliography sections across documents",
"Instruct the model to look only for inline citations, since bibliographies are too long for reliable extraction",
"Convert every document's bibliography into inline citations by hand before sending it for automated extraction",
"Mark the citations field as optional, so the model can skip it whenever the document's format is not inline overall"],
a:[0],e:"Few-shot examples that demonstrate handling of varied document structures (inline citations versus bibliographies) improve extraction across formats. Manual conversion does not scale, and making the field optional just hides the miss."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Source documents in your pipeline express dates as “3/4/24”, “March 4th, 2024”, and “2024-03-04,” but downstream systems need consistent dates. Your schema defines invoice_date as a string. What should you add?",
o:["Explicit normalization rules in the prompt, such as converting all dates to ISO 8601, alongside the schema",
"Nothing more, since a string field typed as a date will normalize every format automatically on its own",
"Nothing in the prompt; extract dates exactly as written and leave normalization to each downstream system",
"A rejection step for any document whose dates are not already in ISO 8601, asking the sender to reformat it"],
a:[0],e:"Include format normalization rules in prompts alongside strict output schemas to handle inconsistent source formatting. Schemas enforce structure, not normalization of meaning."},

{s:6,d:5,q:"Your team runs extraction in production and wants ongoing quality assurance. What is the main purpose of stratified random sampling of high-confidence extractions?",
o:["To measure error rates among high-confidence extractions across segments and to detect novel error patterns",
"To reduce costs by only ever processing a random subset of incoming documents through the extraction model each time",
"To train the model on its own mistakes, since sampled errors are automatically fed back into the model weights",
"To confirm the overall accuracy is 97%, since a simple random sample can only measure the aggregate accuracy"],
a:[0],e:"Stratified random sampling of high-confidence extractions supports ongoing error-rate measurement and detection of novel error patterns across segments (document types, fields)."},

{s:6,d:5,q:"You want to use model confidence to decide which extracted fields in your invoice pipeline need human review. What approach is recommended?",
o:["Have the model output field-level confidence, then calibrate review thresholds on a labeled validation set overall",
"Have the model output one overall confidence per document and send every document below 50% to a human reviewer",
"Trust the raw self-reported confidence scores, since the model knows best how certain it is about each field",
"Skip confidence entirely and send a fixed 10% of documents to review, chosen by the order in which they arrived"],
a:[0],e:"Field-level confidence scores calibrated using labeled validation sets route review attention effectively. Raw self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated, and document-level scores hide which field is weak."},

{s:6,d:5,q:"Your human review team can handle only 5% of daily volume. Which extractions should be prioritized for their review?",
o:["Extractions with low calibrated confidence, or with ambiguous or contradictory source data, go to the reviewers",
"The longest documents go to human review, since document length is the strongest predictor of extraction error over time",
"Documents are reviewed in the order they arrive until the reviewers reach their capacity for that day's work each time",
"Every document from new customers goes to review, regardless of the confidence of the extracted field values each time"],
a:[0],e:"Route extractions with low model confidence or ambiguous or contradictory source documents to human review, prioritizing limited reviewer capacity where errors are most likely."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Which TWO validation failures in your extraction pipeline are likely to be fixed by retrying with the specific error fed back to the model? (Select TWO.)",
o:["The date was extracted as “March 4, 2024” but the schema requires the ISO 8601 format of YYYY-MM-DD",
"The model placed the vendor's tax ID in the invoice_number field, and the validator flagged the format",
"The contract renewal date is required, but it appears only in an amendment that was not provided as input",
"The customer's account number is required, but the scanned invoice page that contained it is entirely blank",
"The purchase order number is required, but the vendor never included one anywhere on its submitted invoice"],
a:[0,1],e:"Retries succeed for format mismatches and structural errors (wrong field placement) because the information is present. They are ineffective when the information is absent from the provided source."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Your extraction already uses tool_use with a strict JSON schema. A teammate asks why you would add Pydantic validation on top. What is the best answer?",
o:["To enforce semantic rules such as totals matching line items, which schema-valid output may still violate",
"To fix JSON syntax errors, which tool_use with a strict schema frequently produces in its structured outputs",
"To reduce token usage, since Pydantic compresses the extracted output before it is sent to downstream systems",
"To replace the tool schema entirely, since Pydantic models can be sent directly to the API in place of tools"],
a:[0],e:"Tool use with strict schemas eliminates syntax errors but not semantic errors (values that do not sum, wrong field placement). Pydantic validation catches these and drives validation-retry loops."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"A finance lead asks you to summarize how the Message Batches API behaves before approving it for overnight extraction. Which statement is accurate?",
o:["It offers about 50% cost savings, can take up to 24 hours, and has no guaranteed latency for individual requests",
"It offers about 50% cost savings and guarantees every request completes within one hour of the batch submission",
"It supports multi-turn tool calling, so tools can be executed mid-request and their results returned in a batch",
"It returns results in exactly the order submitted, so custom_id values are optional for matching up the results"],
a:[0],e:"Batches API: about 50% cost savings, up to a 24-hour processing window, no guaranteed latency SLA, no multi-turn tool calling within a request, and custom_id for correlating request and response pairs."},

{s:6,d:5,q:"Overall extraction accuracy is 97%. Handwritten intake forms are 5% of volume, but accuracy on them is only 70%. What does this imply?",
o:["Aggregate accuracy hides weak segments; validate per document type and keep human review for handwritten forms as well",
"With 97% overall accuracy, the handwritten forms are statistically insignificant and can be fully automated now",
"Exclude handwritten forms from accuracy reporting, so that the published metric reflects typical document quality",
"Raise the confidence threshold for every document type equally, which will fix the handwritten forms specifically"],
a:[0],e:"Aggregate metrics (97% overall) can mask poor performance on specific document types or fields. Validate accuracy by segment before automating, and keep review where performance is weak."},

{s:6,d:5,q:"When extracting obligations from 200-page contracts, the model reliably captures clauses from the first and last sections but frequently misses clauses in the middle. What is the best mitigation?",
o:["Process the contract in focused sections with explicit headers, rather than one pass over all 200 pages overall overall",
"Place the full 200-page contract in one prompt and tell the model to pay equal attention to every single page",
"Truncate the contract to its first and last 50 pages, since those sections contain most of the important clauses",
"Repeat the full contract twice in the same prompt, so that the middle sections appear closer to the beginning"],
a:[0],e:"This is the lost-in-the-middle effect. Organize input into focused sections with explicit headers (and pass findings forward) rather than one long pass. Telling the model to “pay attention” does not change position effects."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"You are designing an extraction schema for vendor invoices. How should you decide which fields are required?",
o:["Fields that every valid document is guaranteed to contain; fields that may be absent should be nullable each time",
"All fields, since required fields force the model to search the document more thoroughly for each value present",
"No fields, since optional fields always produce higher accuracy and avoid any validation failures completely",
"Only numeric fields, since text fields can always be inferred by the model even when they are not in the source"],
a:[0],e:"Mark a field required only when the information is always present. Fields that source documents may lack should be optional or nullable, preventing the model from fabricating values to satisfy required fields."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"You call the Messages API with an extract_invoice tool and tool_choice forcing it. Where do you read the extracted structured data from the response?",
o:["From the input field of the tool_use content block, which contains the arguments matching the tool's schema",
"From the text content block, which the model fills with the JSON after it has finished calling the tool itself",
"From the tool_result block, which the API generates automatically once the model's tool call has completed",
"From the stop_reason field, which contains the extracted JSON whenever a tool call ends the model's response"],
a:[0],e:"With extraction tools, the structured data is the tool call's input: read it from the tool_use block's input field. tool_result blocks are what YOUR code sends back, and stop_reason is just a string like “tool_use.”"},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Extractions from shipping paperwork pass schema validation, but the billing address and shipping address are frequently swapped. What is the best fix?",
o:["Add few-shot examples showing how to tell billing from shipping addresses, plus a semantic check for swaps as well",
"Make both address fields required, since required fields cause the model to read the document more carefully overall",
"Merge both into one address field, since the model cannot distinguish between different address types reliably",
"Switch to tool_choice “any”, since forced tool selection is known to cause values to be placed in wrong fields over time"],
a:[0],e:"Values in the wrong fields are semantic errors that schemas cannot prevent. Few-shot examples showing correct field placement, plus semantic validation with retry feedback, address them. tool_choice does not affect field placement."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Your extraction output sometimes contains invented values. Which TWO schema design choices most directly reduce fabricated values? (Select TWO.)",
o:["Make fields nullable when source documents may not contain the information, so the model can return null",
"Add an “unclear” enum value so the model has a valid option for ambiguous cases instead of forcing a category",
"Make every field required, so that the model must supply a value and cannot quietly skip difficult extractions",
"Remove all enums and use free-text fields everywhere, so that the model is never constrained by a fixed list",
"Add a minimum length constraint to every string field, so that the model cannot return short or empty values"],
a:[0,1],e:"Nullable fields for possibly absent information and an “unclear” enum value for ambiguous cases give the model honest outlets instead of forcing it to invent values. Required fields and length minimums increase fabrication pressure."}
  ]
});
